<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Nabha State</h1><p class="st-meta">1763 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Nabha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the three Phulkian Sikh states of Punjab, whose Maharaja Ripudaman Singh was deposed in 1923 for his nationalist sympathies.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamir-singh-of-nabha">Hamir Singh</a>, a descendant of Phul and kinsman of the rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patiala-state">Patiala</a>, established his own state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in the 1750s and 1760s and founded the town of Nabha. The family held the title of Raja.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nabha accepted British protection with the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cis-sutlej-states">Cis-Sutlej states</a> in 1809. Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaswant-singh-of-nabha">Jaswant Singh</a>'s successor was deposed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> for siding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, but later rulers supported the British in 1857. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripudaman-singh">Ripudaman Singh</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> reform and Indian nationalist causes and was forced to abdicate in 1923, prompting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaito-morcha">Jaito morcha</a> protest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akali-movement">Akalis</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nabha acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947 and in 1948 joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a>, which became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in 1956.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nabha-state.jpg" alt="Nabha State" width="320" height="215"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabha_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1763 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nabha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hamir Singh, Jaswant Singh, Ripudaman Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sikhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nabha</dd></dl></aside></div>
